Sonic 3 A.I.R. on 32-bit ARM (webOS TVs): an unclosed #pragma pack(1)

In SoundDriver.cpp the class Internal opens "#pragma pack(1)" for its
zTrack struct and never closes it, so every member declared after it
(registers, counters, the std::vector of sound chip writes) is packed at
byte offsets. On 32-bit ARM the vector's members are then read with LDM
from an unaligned address, which faults (SIGBUS): the game crashed on the TV
right after the SEGA logo, as the sound driver started
(Internal::zWriteFMIorII -> writeFMI -> vectorAdd). zTrack stays packed
(it overlays the emulated Z80 RAM); the packing now ends after it.

Applied to a copy in the build folder by cmake/PatchUpstream.cmake.

diff -ruN a/Oxygen/oxygenengine/source/oxygen/simulation/sound/SoundDriver.cpp b/Oxygen/oxygenengine/source/oxygen/simulation/sound/SoundDriver.cpp
--- a/Oxygen/oxygenengine/source/oxygen/simulation/sound/SoundDriver.cpp	2026-11-14 04:23:48.269315852 +0000
+++ b/Oxygen/oxygenengine/source/oxygen/simulation/sound/SoundDriver.cpp	2026-11-14 04:23:48.287179258 +0000
@@ -3482,7 +3482,7 @@
 	}
 
 private:
-	#pragma pack(1)
+	#pragma pack(push, 1)
 	struct zTrack
 	{
 		// Playback control bits:
@@ -3574,6 +3574,7 @@
 		uint8 VoicesHigh;			// S&K: 2Bh		; High byte of pointer to track's voices, used only if zUpdatingSFX is set
 		uint32 Stack_top;			// S&K: 2Ch-2Fh	; Track stack; can be used by LoopCounters
 	};
+	#pragma pack(pop)	// Only zTrack is packed: everything after it (the registers, std::vector members) needs its natural alignment
 
 private:
 	uint8 mRegisters[0x20] = { 0 };
